WEBVTT

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:02.100
In my presentation

00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:04.140
I’m going to introduce the subject

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:06.140 
and then present the main points

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:08.160 
and then elaborate on them 

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:10.290
and then give examples, and then…

00:00:010.300 --> 00:00:16.040
Wait. Why are you using “and then,” “and then” all the time?

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:20.110
I don’t know what else to say to move on to the next point

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:24.080
You can use “signpost language.”

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.030
What is “signpost language”? 

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:31.150
Isn’t a signpost the signal along the road to direct the way?

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.090
Yes. So signpost language means the words and phrases

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.090 
that guide the audience through the presentation

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:43.080
They indicate what has happened so far

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.100 
and what is going to happen next

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:049.240
Do we use different signposts

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:51.250
throughout the whole presentation?

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:55.190
There is different signpost language for the introduction

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.250
the body and the conclusion.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.090
Signposting can be effective 

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.090
in keeping your audience focused

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.080 
and knowing where you are in the presentation

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.090
What are some signpost phrases 

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.140
I can use in the introduction?

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.240
Phrases like first of all

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:15.030
to begin with, let’s start by…

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:20.150
And I can use phrases like firstly, secondly

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:23.000 
and lastly to indicate the order

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.190
Correct. And when moving on to the next point

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:27.110 
you can say

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.100
I’ve talked about…, now let’s move on to…

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:34.100
How about elaborating and giving examples? 

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.160
Is there anything else we can say

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:40.170 
besides “in more detail” and “for instance”?

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.100
Sentence patterns like

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.020
“let me tell you more about…”

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.170 
or “I’d like to expand on…” 

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:49.260
can be used to paraphrase or for elaboration

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.170
I can also say “To illustrate this point…”

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.160
or “A good example of this is…” to give examples

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.050
Some key words we can use in the conclusion are 

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.030
summarize, conclude and recap.

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:09.040
To invite questions, some useful phrases are 

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:12.070
“Please feel free to ask questions”

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:16.180 
and “I am happy to answer any questions.”

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:20.130
To put it simply, the audience won’t get “lost”

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:24.000 
in the presentation when signpost language is used.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:26.230
And I don’t have to repeat

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:30.100 
“and then … and then … and then …” all the time.
